// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 2): technology and space.
ADDART({id:"vacuumcleaner",t:"Vacuum Cleaners",c:"Technology",i:"🧹",w:"Vacuum cleaner",r:["washingmachine","motors","robot","elec","smarthome"],
p:[["Sucking up dust","Vacuum cleaners use a fan to create suction that pulls in dirt."],["Horse-drawn beginnings","Early vacuum cleaners were so big they were parked outside houses."],["Robots at home","Robot vacuums now clean floors by themselves."]],
l:"A vacuum cleaner is a machine that removes dust and dirt from floors, carpets and furniture by suction. An electric motor spins a fan that lowers the air pressure inside the machine, so outside air rushes in through a nozzle, carrying dirt with it; the dirt is trapped in a bag, a container or filters, and the cleaned air is blown out. Before vacuum cleaners, people swept with brooms, beat rugs outdoors and used hand-pushed carpet sweepers. In 1901, British engineer Hubert Cecil Booth built a huge gasoline-powered vacuum machine pulled by horses, which was parked outside buildings with long hoses fed through the windows. In 1907, American janitor James Murray Spangler invented a portable electric upright cleaner and sold the patent to the Hoover company, whose name became a word for vacuuming in Britain. Later developments included bagless cyclonic cleaners, popularized by James Dyson in the 1990s, cordless stick vacuums and robot vacuums such as the Roomba, introduced in 2002.",
s:[["How suction works","The fan does not create a true vacuum but a partial one, where air pressure is lower than outside. Brushes in the cleaner head loosen dirt from carpets. Filters, including HEPA filters, which trap at least 99.97 percent of very small particles, help people with allergies."],["Booth’s ‘Puffing Billy’","After seeing a machine that blew dust off train seats, Booth tested sucking dust instead by putting a handkerchief over his mouth and breathing in through it on a chair. His company’s large vacuum vans cleaned Westminster Abbey’s carpets before the coronation of King Edward VII in 1902."],["Cyclonic vacuums","James Dyson built 5,127 prototypes over five years before perfecting his bagless cyclone vacuum, which spins air so fast that dust is flung out into a clear bin. His DC01 went on sale in the United Kingdom in 1993 and became a best-seller."],["Robot vacuums","Robot vacuums navigate rooms using bumpers, infrared sensors, cameras or laser scanners and map homes with software. Many return to a charging dock and empty their own bins. Some people have raised privacy concerns about robot vacuums that send maps and images of homes to company servers."]],
k:[["Works by","Suction from a motor-driven fan"],["First powered vacuum","Hubert Cecil Booth, 1901"],["Portable upright","James Murray Spangler, 1907"],["Bagless cyclonic","Dyson DC01, 1993"],["First popular robot vacuum","Roomba, 2002"]],
d:[["1860","Hand-powered carpet sweeper patented"],["1901","Booth builds a horse-drawn vacuum"],["1907","Spangler invents the portable electric vacuum"],["1908","Hoover begins selling Spangler’s design"],["1993","Dyson DC01 goes on sale"],["2002","Roomba robot vacuum introduced"]],
s2:[["Hoovering","In the United Kingdom and Ireland, ‘hoover’ is used as a verb meaning to vacuum, after the Hoover company, even when the machine is made by another brand. Similar brand names that became words include ‘thermos’ and ‘escalator’."],["Vacuums in space","Astronauts on the International Space Station use small vacuum cleaners to clean filters and collect floating crumbs and dust, which do not settle on surfaces in weightlessness."]],
sp:["Dust and allergies","Household dust contains skin flakes, fibers, pollen, soil and tiny dust mites. Regular vacuuming with a good filter reduces allergens, but poorly sealed cleaners can blow fine particles back into the air."],
g:[["Suction","Pulling by creating lower pressure"],["HEPA filter","A high-efficiency filter for tiny particles"],["Cyclonic","Using spinning air to separate dust"],["Prototype","An early test version of an invention"]],
f:["The first powered vacuum was pulled by horses.","Dyson built 5,127 prototypes.","Robot vacuums map rooms to clean them."],
fun:"Hubert Cecil Booth’s horse-drawn vacuum cleaner was so noisy that he was sued by cab drivers whose horses it frightened."});

ADDART({id:"sonar",t:"Sonar",c:"Technology",i:"🔊",w:"Sonar",r:["radar","sound","submarine","titanic","trench","echolocation"],
p:[["Seeing with sound","Sonar uses sound waves to find objects underwater."],["Born from disaster and war","The Titanic sinking and submarine warfare spurred its invention."],["Mapping the seafloor","Sonar has mapped the ocean floor and found shipwrecks."]],
l:"Sonar, short for ‘sound navigation and ranging’, is a technology that uses sound waves to detect, locate and identify objects underwater. Sound travels much farther through water than light or radio waves, so sonar is the main way of ‘seeing’ in the ocean. Active sonar sends out a pulse of sound, called a ping, and listens for echoes; the time an echo takes to return shows how far away the object is, since sound travels at about 1,500 meters per second in seawater. Passive sonar only listens, detecting sounds made by ships, submarines or animals. Sonar was developed in the early 20th century, spurred by the sinking of the Titanic in 1912 and by submarine warfare in World War I. Today it is used by navies, fishing boats to find schools of fish, scientists to map the seafloor and study marine life, and searchers to locate shipwrecks and lost aircraft. Dolphins, whales and bats use a natural form of sonar called echolocation.",
s:[["How it works","A sonar transducer turns electrical signals into sound pulses and turns returning echoes back into signals. Multibeam sonars send out a fan of hundreds of beams to map wide strips of the seafloor in detail, while side-scan sonar creates picture-like images of objects lying on the bottom."],["History","After the Titanic struck an iceberg, Canadian inventor Reginald Fessenden developed an underwater sound device that detected an iceberg about 3 kilometers away in 1914. During World War I, French physicist Paul Langevin used quartz crystals to produce ultrasonic pulses for detecting submarines. Britain called its system ASDIC, and the American term ‘sonar’ was adopted in the 1940s."],["Mapping the ocean","Echo sounders revealed the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and deep trenches. Marie Tharp used sonar soundings to draw the first maps of the ocean floor in the 1950s, which helped prove plate tectonics. The Seabed 2030 project aims to map the whole seafloor; by 2024, about a quarter had been mapped in detail."],["Sonar and whales","Powerful military sonar has been linked to mass strandings of beaked whales, which may surface too quickly in panic. Navies now try to avoid using strong sonar in sensitive areas, and courts have sometimes limited exercises."]],
k:[["Stands for","Sound navigation and ranging"],["Active sonar","Sends pings and listens for echoes"],["Passive sonar","Only listens"],["Speed of sound in seawater","About 1,500 m/s"],["Natural version","Echolocation"]],
d:[["1912","Titanic sinks, spurring iceberg-detection research"],["1914","Fessenden detects an iceberg with sound"],["1918","Langevin’s ultrasonic submarine detection"],["1950s","Marie Tharp maps the seafloor using sonar data"],["1985","Titanic wreck found using side-scan sonar"],["2017","Seabed 2030 project launched"]],
s2:[["Finding the Titanic","In 1985, a French–American team led by Robert Ballard and Jean-Louis Michel found the Titanic about 3,800 meters deep using side-scan sonar and cameras towed behind a ship. Ballard had been searching partly as cover for a secret US Navy mission to inspect sunken nuclear submarines."],["Fish finders","Many fishing boats and even recreational anglers use small sonars called fish finders, which show fish as marks on a screen. Scientists use similar sonars to estimate fish stocks and track krill."]],
sp:["Echolocation","Dolphins produce rapid clicks and listen for echoes, which let them detect a small ball from about 100 meters away and even sense objects buried in sand. Bats use echolocation in air to catch insects in the dark."],
g:[["Ping","A short pulse of sound sent by sonar"],["Transducer","A device that converts electricity to sound and back"],["Echo","A reflected sound"],["Multibeam","Sonar using many beams at once"]],
f:["Sound travels about 1,500 meters a second in seawater.","Sonar helped find the Titanic.","Only about a quarter of the seafloor is mapped in detail."],
fun:"During World War II, sonar operators were puzzled by a ‘false bottom’ in the ocean that rose at night; it turned out to be huge layers of tiny animals migrating up and down."});

ADDART({id:"trafficlights",t:"Traffic Lights",c:"Technology",i:"🚦",w:"Traffic light",r:["cars","roads","lightbulb","smartcities","leds"],
p:[["Red, yellow, green","Traffic lights control the flow of vehicles and pedestrians at intersections."],["First in London","The first traffic signal, gas-lit, was installed in London in 1868."],["Getting smarter","Modern signals use sensors and computers to adjust timing."]],
l:"Traffic lights, also called traffic signals, are signaling devices at road intersections and crossings that control the flow of vehicles, cyclists and pedestrians using colored lights. The standard system uses red for stop, green for go and yellow or amber for caution. The first traffic signal was installed outside the Houses of Parliament in London in 1868; designed by railway engineer John Peake Knight, it used semaphore arms by day and red and green gas lamps at night, but it exploded in January 1869, injuring the police officer operating it. Electric traffic lights appeared in the United States in the 1910s; Cleveland, Ohio, installed one in 1914, and Detroit introduced the first three-color, four-way signal in 1920. Automatic signals with timers soon replaced police officers. Today, traffic lights use energy-efficient LEDs and are often linked to sensors, cameras and computer systems that adjust timing to traffic. They have helped reduce collisions and organize the growing number of cars in cities.",
s:[["Why red, yellow and green?","Railways used red for danger and, later, green for safe before roads did. Red light is easy to see from a distance. Yellow was added to warn drivers that the signal was about to change. Arrow signals, pedestrian figures and countdown timers were added later."],["Garrett Morgan","Garrett Morgan, an African American inventor and businessman in Cleveland, patented a traffic signal in 1923 with a ‘stop all’ position that halted traffic in all directions so pedestrians could cross. He sold the rights to General Electric."],["Smart signals","Many cities use sensors in the road or cameras to detect waiting vehicles. Coordinated signals create ‘green waves’ along main roads. Some systems give priority to buses, trams and emergency vehicles, and researchers test signals that communicate directly with connected and self-driving cars."],["Rules around the world","In some countries, drivers may turn right (or left, where traffic drives on the left) on red after stopping. In Japan, some green lights look blue, partly because traditional Japanese uses the word ao for both green and blue. Pedestrian signals often beep to help blind people cross."]],
k:[["Colors","Red (stop), yellow (caution), green (go)"],["First signal","London, 1868"],["First electric signal","Cleveland, 1914"],["First three-color four-way signal","Detroit, 1920"],["Modern lights","LEDs and sensors"]],
d:[["1868","First traffic signal installed in London"],["1869","London signal explodes"],["1914","Electric traffic light installed in Cleveland"],["1920","Detroit introduces a three-color, four-way signal"],["1923","Garrett Morgan patents a traffic signal"],["1990s","LED traffic lights come into use"]],
s2:[["The green man","Pedestrian signals with walking and standing figures spread from the mid-20th century. East Germany’s Ampelmännchen, a little man in a hat designed in 1961, became so loved that he survived German reunification and is now a popular symbol of Berlin."],["Roundabouts instead","Many traffic engineers prefer roundabouts to traffic lights at some intersections because they slow traffic and reduce serious crashes. Some towns have removed traffic lights entirely in ‘shared space’ experiments."]],
sp:["The exploding signal","London’s first traffic signal was operated by a police officer who turned a lever. Less than a month after installation, a gas leak caused the lamp to explode, and the officer was badly burned. Traffic signals did not return to London until 1926."],
g:[["Intersection","A place where roads cross"],["Semaphore","Signaling with movable arms"],["Green wave","A series of lights timed to turn green in sequence"],["Sensor","A device that detects something"]],
f:["The first traffic signal was in London in 1868.","London’s first signal exploded.","Some green lights in Japan look blue."],
fun:"There are no traffic lights in the whole country of Bhutan; police officers direct traffic at busy junctions in the capital, Thimphu."});

ADDART({id:"vrar",t:"Virtual and Augmented Reality",c:"Technology",i:"🥽",w:"Virtual reality",r:["videogames","smartphone","compvision","wearables","ai"],
p:[["Digital worlds","Virtual reality places users in a computer-generated world."],["Digital layers","Augmented reality adds digital images to the real world."],["Many uses","VR and AR are used in games, training, medicine and design."]],
l:"Virtual reality (VR) is technology that immerses users in a computer-generated environment, usually through a headset that covers the eyes, shows separate images to each eye to create depth and tracks head movements so the view changes as the user looks around. Augmented reality (AR) overlays digital images and information onto the real world, seen through a phone screen, glasses or a headset; mixed reality blends the two. Early experiments included Morton Heilig’s Sensorama booth of 1962 and Ivan Sutherland’s 1968 head-mounted display, nicknamed the ‘Sword of Damocles’ because it hung from the ceiling. VR became affordable for consumers with headsets such as the Oculus Rift in 2016, and the game Pokémon Go brought AR to hundreds of millions of phone users the same year. Today VR and AR are used for gaming, flight and surgical training, architecture, education and remote work. Challenges include motion sickness, high costs, privacy and the effects of long use on health.",
s:[["How VR works","A VR headset contains small screens and lenses that fill the user’s field of view. Sensors and cameras track the position and rotation of the head and hands many times a second. If the image lags behind head movements, users can feel sick, so headsets need fast displays and processing."],["How AR works","AR devices use cameras and sensors to understand the surroundings, detecting surfaces and objects so digital images appear fixed in place. Phone apps let people preview furniture in their homes or translate signs. Head-up displays in aircraft and some cars show information on the windshield."],["Uses","Pilots, surgeons, soldiers and factory workers train with VR simulations. Architects walk through buildings before they are built. Therapists use VR to treat phobias and post-traumatic stress, and museums recreate historical sites. VR is also used to help people manage pain."],["Concerns","Many people experience motion sickness or eye strain in VR. Headsets collect detailed data about movements, eyes and surroundings, raising privacy questions. Manufacturers recommend that young children not use headsets, and researchers study how long use affects vision and wellbeing."]],
k:[["VR","Immersion in a digital world"],["AR","Digital images added to the real world"],["Early headset","Sutherland’s ‘Sword of Damocles’, 1968"],["Consumer VR boom","2016 (Oculus Rift, HTC Vive)"],["AR hit","Pokémon Go, 2016"]],
d:[["1838","Charles Wheatstone invents the stereoscope"],["1962","Sensorama booth patented"],["1968","Ivan Sutherland’s head-mounted display"],["2016","Oculus Rift and Pokémon Go released"],["2019","Oculus Quest standalone headset released"],["2024","Apple Vision Pro released"]],
s2:[["The stereoscope","In 1838, Charles Wheatstone showed that two slightly different pictures viewed one by each eye create an illusion of depth. Stereoscopes and later View-Master toys used this idea, which is the basis of VR headsets."],["The metaverse","In the early 2020s, some technology companies promoted the idea of a ‘metaverse’, a shared virtual world for work and play. Facebook renamed itself Meta in 2021 to focus on it, though public interest grew more slowly than predicted."]],
sp:["Pokémon Go","Released in July 2016, Pokémon Go let players catch virtual creatures that appeared on their phone screens in real places. It was downloaded more than 500 million times in its first year and sent crowds into parks and streets, along with some accidents."],
g:[["Immersion","Feeling surrounded by an environment"],["Headset","A device worn over the eyes"],["Motion sickness","Nausea caused by mismatched senses"],["Mixed reality","Blending virtual and real objects"]],
f:["The first VR headset hung from the ceiling.","Pokémon Go brought AR to millions in 2016.","VR is used to train surgeons and pilots."],
fun:"The stereoscope, invented in 1838, worked on the same principle as modern VR headsets, showing a slightly different picture to each eye."});

ADDART({id:"passwords",t:"Passwords and Passkeys",c:"Technology",i:"🔑",w:"Password",r:["cybersecurity","crypto","hacking","dataprivacy","locks","webcookies"],
p:[["Secret words","Passwords prove who you are when you log in."],["Easy to steal","Weak and reused passwords are a major cause of hacking."],["Beyond passwords","Passkeys and two-factor authentication make accounts safer."]],
l:"A password is a secret string of characters used to prove a person’s identity when logging in to a computer, website or device. Passwords have ancient roots in the watchwords used by soldiers, but computer passwords began in 1961 with the Compatible Time-Sharing System at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, which let many users share one computer. Today people may have dozens or hundreds of online accounts, and many choose weak passwords, such as ‘123456’, or reuse the same password on several sites, so that one data breach exposes many accounts. Criminals use guessing programs, stolen password lists and phishing messages to steal them. Security experts recommend long, unique passwords stored in a password manager, along with two-factor authentication, which requires a second proof such as a code from a phone. Passkeys, introduced widely from 2022, replace passwords with cryptographic keys stored on a device and unlocked by a fingerprint, face or PIN, and are much harder to steal.",
s:[["How passwords are stored","Well-designed systems do not store passwords directly. Instead they store a ‘hash’, the result of a one-way mathematical function, so even if the database is stolen, the passwords cannot easily be read. Adding random data called salt makes hashes harder to crack."],["Strong passwords","Length matters more than complexity: a passphrase of several random words is easier to remember and harder to guess than a short password with symbols. Guidance from the US National Institute of Standards and Technology no longer recommends forcing people to change passwords regularly unless they may be compromised."],["Two-factor authentication","Two-factor authentication combines something you know, such as a password, with something you have, such as a phone or security key, or something you are, such as a fingerprint. Codes sent by text message are better than nothing but can be intercepted; authenticator apps and hardware keys are safer."],["Passkeys","Passkeys use public-key cryptography: a website stores a public key, while the private key stays on the user’s device. Because nothing secret is typed or sent, passkeys cannot be phished or leaked in a server breach. Apple, Google and Microsoft announced support for passkeys in 2022."]],
k:[["First computer passwords","MIT, 1961"],["Most common password","‘123456’"],["Safer storage","Hashing with salt"],["Extra protection","Two-factor authentication"],["Password replacement","Passkeys (from 2022)"]],
d:[["1961","MIT’s time-sharing system introduces computer passwords"],["1962","First known password theft, at MIT"],["1970s","Password hashing comes into use"],["2004","Bill Gates predicts the death of the password"],["2013","Yahoo breach exposes data of 3 billion accounts"],["2022","Tech companies commit to passkeys"]],
s2:[["The first password theft","In 1962, MIT researcher Allan Scherr printed out the file of all passwords on the time-sharing system so he could get more computer time than his limit allowed, and shared it with others. It is often called the first computer password theft."],["Password managers","Password managers generate and store strong, unique passwords for each account, protected by one master password. They make good security easier, though users must protect the master password and turn on two-factor authentication."]],
sp:["World Password Day","World Password Day, held on the first Thursday of May since 2013, reminds people to check and strengthen their passwords and turn on extra security."],
g:[["Hash","The result of a one-way mathematical scramble"],["Phishing","Tricking people into revealing secrets"],["Two-factor authentication","Logging in with two kinds of proof"],["Passkey","A login method using cryptographic keys"]],
f:["Computer passwords began at MIT in 1961.","Length matters more than complexity in passwords.","Passkeys cannot be phished."],
fun:"A study of leaked passwords repeatedly finds ‘123456’ and ‘password’ among the most common, used by millions of people."});

ADDART({id:"headphones",t:"Headphones and Loudspeakers",c:"Technology",i:"🎧",w:"Headphones",r:["sound","ear","radio","streaming","smartphone"],
p:[["Turning signals into sound","Speakers and headphones turn electrical signals into sound waves."],["Moving coils","Most use a coil and magnet to vibrate a cone or diaphragm."],["Music on the move","The Walkman and wireless earbuds changed how people listen."]],
l:"Loudspeakers and headphones are devices that convert electrical audio signals into sound. In the most common design, the moving-coil driver, an electrical signal flows through a coil of wire attached to a cone or diaphragm and placed near a magnet; the changing magnetic force pushes the coil back and forth, making the cone vibrate and push the air into sound waves. Early telephones of the 1870s used simple speakers in their earpieces, and in 1910 American inventor Nathaniel Baldwin made headphones in his kitchen that he sold to the US Navy. The moving-coil loudspeaker, invented by Chester Rice and Edward Kellogg in 1925, made radios, record players and cinemas sound fuller. Sony’s Walkman cassette player, launched in 1979, made personal listening with headphones a global habit, and wireless earbuds such as Apple’s AirPods in 2016 spread rapidly. Noise-canceling headphones use microphones and opposing sound waves to quiet background noise. Listening at high volume for long periods can permanently damage hearing.",
s:[["Types of speakers","Large woofers reproduce low bass sounds, and small tweeters reproduce high sounds; many speakers combine several drivers. Electrostatic and planar magnetic speakers use thin films instead of cones. Sound systems in cinemas and concerts use many speakers to fill large spaces."],["Kinds of headphones","Over-ear headphones cover the ears, on-ear headphones rest on them, and earbuds or in-ear monitors fit in the ear canal. Bone-conduction headphones send vibrations through the cheekbones, leaving the ears open to hear traffic."],["Noise canceling","Active noise-canceling headphones use microphones to pick up outside noise and produce a mirror-image sound wave that cancels it out, a process called destructive interference. Bose developed noise-canceling headsets for pilots in the 1980s, and they became popular with travelers."],["Protecting hearing","The World Health Organization estimates that more than a billion young people are at risk of hearing loss from unsafe listening. Experts suggest the ‘60/60 rule’: no more than 60 percent volume for 60 minutes at a time. Many phones now warn users about loud listening."]],
k:[["Main design","Moving-coil driver"],["Early headphones","Nathaniel Baldwin, 1910"],["Moving-coil loudspeaker","Rice and Kellogg, 1925"],["Personal stereo","Sony Walkman, 1979"],["Wireless earbuds boom","From 2016"]],
d:[["1876","Telephone patented, with a speaker in the earpiece"],["1910","Baldwin makes headphones in his kitchen"],["1925","Moving-coil loudspeaker invented"],["1958","First stereo headphones sold"],["1979","Sony Walkman launched"],["2016","Apple AirPods released"]],
s2:[["The Walkman","Sony’s Walkman TPS-L2 went on sale on 1 July 1979. Engineers built it partly so company co-founder Masaru Ibuka could listen to opera on long flights. More than 200 million Walkmans were sold, and it made headphones a fashion item."],["Bluetooth","Most wireless headphones use Bluetooth, a short-range radio technology introduced in 1999 and named after Harald Bluetooth, a 10th-century Danish king who united tribes, just as the technology unites devices."]],
sp:["Kitchen headphones","Nathaniel Baldwin built his first headphones at his kitchen table in Utah. When he offered them to the US Navy, it was so impressed that it ordered a hundred sets, and he began manufacturing them in his kitchen."],
g:[["Driver","The part of a speaker that makes sound"],["Diaphragm","A thin vibrating sheet"],["Destructive interference","When waves cancel each other out"],["Bluetooth","A short-range wireless technology"]],
f:["The Walkman went on sale in 1979.","Noise-canceling uses opposing sound waves.","Bluetooth is named after a Danish king."],
fun:"A loudspeaker can work as a microphone in reverse: speak into a speaker, and it will produce a tiny electrical signal."});

ADDART({id:"reusablerockets",t:"Reusable Rockets",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"Reusable launch vehicle",r:["rocket","commspace","spaceports","starship","sat"],
p:[["Flying again and again","Reusable rockets land after launch so they can fly again."],["Cutting costs","Reuse has made launching satellites far cheaper."],["Falcon 9 records","One Falcon 9 booster has flown 37 times."]],
l:"A reusable rocket is a launch vehicle, or part of one, that is recovered after flight and flown again, instead of being discarded. For most of the space age, rockets were used only once, like throwing away an airliner after every trip, which made spaceflight extremely expensive. The Space Shuttle, first launched in 1981, was partly reusable, but refurbishing it between flights was slow and costly. SpaceX made reuse routine: on 21 December 2015, a Falcon 9 first stage landed upright back at Cape Canaveral, and in March 2017 a booster flew for a second time. Falcon 9 boosters now land on ships at sea or landing pads, and in August 2026 one booster made its 37th flight. Reuse, together with mass production, has greatly reduced launch costs and helped make large satellite constellations possible. Blue Origin landed its New Shepard booster in 2015 and its large New Glenn booster in 2025, and SpaceX catches its giant Starship boosters with tower arms. Chinese companies are also developing reusable rockets.",
s:[["How landing works","After separating from the upper stage, a reusable booster flips around, fires some engines to slow down and steer, deploys grid fins to guide its fall through the atmosphere, and makes a final landing burn, touching down on legs. Computers make thousands of adjustments to land precisely."],["The Space Shuttle","The Space Shuttle orbiter returned to Earth like a glider, and its solid rocket boosters were recovered from the ocean, but its large external tank was discarded. Each orbiter needed months of inspection and repair between flights, and two orbiters were lost, in 1986 and 2003."],["Falcon 9","Since its first landing, the Falcon 9 first stage has landed hundreds of times. SpaceX also recovers the fairings that protect payloads, catching them from the sea. Reused boosters have carried astronauts to the International Space Station."],["Catching rockets","SpaceX’s Starship launch towers have mechanical ‘chopstick’ arms that catch descending Super Heavy boosters, first achieved on 13 October 2024. In November 2026, the tower caught a Starship upper stage returning from orbit for the first time, a step toward fully reusable rockets."]],
k:[["Partly reusable pioneer","Space Shuttle (1981)"],["First orbital booster landing","Falcon 9, 21 December 2015"],["First reflight","Falcon 9, March 2017"],["Most flights by one booster","37 (August 2026)"],["First tower catch","Super Heavy, 13 October 2024"]],
d:[["1981","Space Shuttle makes its first flight"],["1993","DC-X test rocket makes vertical landings"],["2015","New Shepard and Falcon 9 boosters land"],["2017","First reflight of an orbital booster"],["2024","First booster caught by tower arms"],["2025","New Glenn booster lands at sea"],["2026","Starship upper stage caught after orbital flight"]],
s2:[["Why reuse saves money","The first stage is the largest and most expensive part of a rocket. Reusing it means the main costs per launch are fuel, refurbishment and the upper stage. Launch prices per kilogram to orbit fell sharply in the 2010s and 2020s."],["Concerns","Cheap, frequent launches have filled low Earth orbit with satellites, raising concerns about space debris, astronomy and pollution from rocket exhaust in the upper atmosphere. Scientists are studying these effects."]],
sp:["Grid fins","Grid fins are lattice-shaped panels near the top of a Falcon 9 booster that unfold during descent. Like the steering fins of a dart, they help guide the booster through the air toward its landing site."],
g:[["Booster","The first stage of a rocket"],["Landing burn","Firing engines to slow down before touchdown"],["Fairing","A nose cone protecting a payload"],["Refurbishment","Repairing and preparing for reuse"]],
f:["Falcon 9 boosters land upright.","One booster has flown 37 times.","Starship boosters are caught by tower arms."],
fun:"SpaceX’s drone ships at sea are named after spaceships from Iain M. Banks’s science fiction novels, such as ‘Of Course I Still Love You’."});

ADDART({id:"spacelaw",t:"Space Law",c:"Space Exploration",i:"⚖️",w:"Space law",r:["moonbases","un","spaceagencies","spacejunk","artemis"],
p:[["Rules for space","Space law is the body of agreements that governs activities in outer space."],["The Outer Space Treaty","The 1967 treaty says no country can own the Moon or other celestial bodies."],["New questions","Mining, megaconstellations and Moon bases raise new legal debates."]],
l:"Space law is the set of international treaties, national laws and guidelines that govern human activities in outer space. Its foundation is the Outer Space Treaty, which opened for signature on 27 January 1967 and has been joined by more than 110 countries, including all major spacefaring nations. It declares that space is free for exploration and use by all countries, that no nation can claim sovereignty over the Moon or other celestial bodies, that nuclear weapons and other weapons of mass destruction may not be placed in orbit or on celestial bodies, and that countries are responsible for activities by their governments and companies, and liable for damage caused by their space objects. Further United Nations treaties cover rescuing astronauts (1968), liability for damage (1972), registering space objects (1975) and the Moon (1979), though the Moon Agreement has few members. The United Nations Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space, set up in 1959, discusses new rules. Recent issues include mining resources from the Moon and asteroids, space debris, crowded orbits and military activities in space.",
s:[["The Outer Space Treaty","Negotiated during the Cold War between the United States, the Soviet Union and others, the treaty aimed to keep the space race peaceful. Astronauts are described as ‘envoys of mankind’, and countries must help astronauts in distress. The treaty entered into force on 10 October 1967."],["Liability","Under the 1972 Liability Convention, a launching country must pay for damage its space objects cause on Earth or to aircraft. It has been formally used only once: after the Soviet nuclear-powered satellite Cosmos 954 crashed in northern Canada in 1978, spreading radioactive debris, the Soviet Union paid Canada 3 million Canadian dollars."],["Space resources","The Outer Space Treaty bans claiming territory but does not clearly say whether companies may own resources they extract. The United States passed a law in 2015 allowing citizens to own space resources, followed by Luxembourg, the United Arab Emirates and Japan. Critics argue that such laws could amount to claiming parts of space."],["The Artemis Accords","Launched by the United States in October 2020, the Artemis Accords are non-binding principles for exploring the Moon and beyond, covering transparency, sharing scientific data, protecting historic sites such as Apollo landing sites, and ‘safety zones’ around operations. More than 75 countries had signed by 2026. China and Russia have not joined and are developing their own lunar partnership."]],
k:[["Main treaty","Outer Space Treaty (1967)"],["Key principle","No national ownership of celestial bodies"],["UN committee","COPUOS (1959)"],["Only liability claim","Cosmos 954, Canada (1978)"],["Artemis Accords","Launched 2020; 75+ signatories"]],
d:[["1959","UN Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space set up"],["1967","Outer Space Treaty enters into force"],["1972","Liability Convention adopted"],["1978","Cosmos 954 crashes in Canada"],["1979","Moon Agreement adopted"],["2015","US law allows ownership of space resources"],["2020","Artemis Accords launched"]],
s2:[["Who owns the Moon?","No one: the Outer Space Treaty forbids national claims. Over the years, some people have claimed to sell plots of land on the Moon, but these sales have no legal value. Footprints, flags and equipment left by the Apollo missions remain the property of the United States."],["Weapons in space","The treaty bans weapons of mass destruction in orbit but not conventional weapons or satellites with military uses. Anti-satellite missile tests, which create debris, have led many countries to pledge not to conduct them, and the UN has debated new rules to prevent an arms race in space."]],
sp:["The Bogotá Declaration","In 1976, eight equatorial countries, including Colombia, Ecuador and Indonesia, signed the Bogotá Declaration claiming the parts of geostationary orbit above their territories. Other countries rejected the claim as conflicting with the Outer Space Treaty."],
g:[["Treaty","A formal agreement between countries"],["Sovereignty","Supreme authority over a territory"],["Liability","Legal responsibility for damage"],["Celestial body","A natural object in space, such as a planet or moon"]],
f:["No country can own the Moon.","The Liability Convention has been used only once.","More than 75 countries have signed the Artemis Accords."],
fun:"The Outer Space Treaty calls astronauts ‘envoys of mankind’, and countries must return astronauts who land on their territory by accident."});

ADDART({id:"sunspots",t:"Sunspots and the Solar Cycle",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌞",w:"Sunspot",r:["sun","spaceweather","aurora","galileo","scope"],
p:[["Dark spots on the Sun","Sunspots are cooler, darker areas caused by strong magnetic fields."],["An 11-year cycle","The number of sunspots rises and falls about every 11 years."],["Solar storms","Active periods bring more solar flares, auroras and space weather."]],
l:"Sunspots are temporary dark patches on the Sun’s surface, the photosphere, where intense magnetic fields slow the flow of heat from inside, making them about 1,500°C cooler than their surroundings. They still glow brightly but look dark by contrast. Sunspots range from small specks to groups many times wider than Earth and last from hours to months. Their number rises and falls in a solar cycle lasting about 11 years, from solar minimum, when there may be no spots for weeks, to solar maximum, with many spots, powerful solar flares and coronal mass ejections. These eruptions can cause geomagnetic storms that create bright auroras but can also disrupt satellites, radio communications, GPS and power grids. Sunspots were observed with the naked eye in ancient China and studied with telescopes by Galileo and others from 1610. The current cycle, Solar Cycle 25, reached its maximum in October 2024. Viewing the Sun directly is dangerous; sunspots should only be observed with proper solar filters or projection.",
s:[["What causes sunspots","The Sun’s gas is electrically charged and rotates faster at the equator than near the poles, twisting its magnetic field. Bundles of magnetic field rise to the surface, where they block heat and form sunspots, usually in pairs of opposite magnetic polarity. A sunspot has a dark center called the umbra and a lighter outer region called the penumbra."],["The solar cycle","German astronomer Samuel Heinrich Schwabe discovered the sunspot cycle in 1843 after counting spots for 17 years. The cycles are numbered from 1755. Every 11 years the Sun’s magnetic poles flip, so the full magnetic cycle lasts about 22 years. Solar Cycle 25 peaked with a smoothed sunspot number of about 161."],["The Maunder Minimum","Between about 1645 and 1715, very few sunspots were seen, a period called the Maunder Minimum. It overlapped with the coldest part of the Little Ice Age in Europe, though scientists think volcanic eruptions and other factors also played a large role in the cold."],["Solar storms","On 1 September 1859, astronomer Richard Carrington saw a brilliant solar flare. The following geomagnetic storm, the Carrington Event, produced auroras as far south as the Caribbean and sparked fires in telegraph offices. In May 2024, the strongest storm in two decades brought auroras to much of the world."]],
k:[["Temperature","About 1,500°C cooler than surroundings"],["Cycle length","About 11 years"],["Cycle discovered","Samuel Heinrich Schwabe, 1843"],["Solar Cycle 25 maximum","October 2024"],["Quiet period","Maunder Minimum (1645–1715)"]],
d:[["28 BC","Chinese astronomers record sunspots"],["1610–1613","Galileo and others observe sunspots by telescope"],["1645–1715","Maunder Minimum"],["1843","Schwabe discovers the sunspot cycle"],["1859","Carrington Event"],["2024","Solar Cycle 25 reaches maximum"]],
s2:[["Sunspots and climate","The Sun is slightly brighter at solar maximum, because bright regions called faculae outweigh the dark spots, but the change is only about 0.1 percent. Scientists have found that the solar cycle does not explain the recent warming of Earth’s climate."],["Predicting the cycle","Scientists predicted that Solar Cycle 25 would be weak, peaking at about 115, but it turned out much stronger. Forecasting the strength of cycles remains difficult, though magnetic fields at the Sun’s poles during minimum give clues."]],
sp:["Watching safely","Never look at the Sun directly or through binoculars or a telescope without a certified solar filter, which can cause permanent eye damage. Projecting the Sun’s image through a telescope or pinhole onto white card is a safe way to see sunspots."],
g:[["Photosphere","The visible surface of the Sun"],["Solar flare","A sudden burst of energy from the Sun"],["Coronal mass ejection","A huge cloud of solar material thrown into space"],["Umbra","The dark center of a sunspot"]],
f:["Sunspots are cooler than the rest of the Sun’s surface.","The sunspot cycle lasts about 11 years.","Solar Cycle 25 peaked in October 2024."],
fun:"A large sunspot group can be many times wider than Earth, and the biggest can be seen without a telescope at sunset through haze, with proper eye protection."});

ADDART({id:"impactcraters",t:"Impact Craters",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🕳️",w:"Impact crater",r:["meteors","moon","planetdefense","dino","comet"],
p:[["Scars from space","Impact craters form when asteroids or comets hit a planet or moon."],["Earth’s hidden craters","About 200 impact craters are confirmed on Earth."],["Shaping history","The Chicxulub impact helped end the age of dinosaurs."]],
l:"An impact crater is a roughly circular depression formed when a meteoroid, asteroid or comet strikes the surface of a planet, moon or other solid body at high speed. Because impactors travel at tens of kilometers per second, they release enormous energy, vaporizing rock and blasting out a crater typically about 10 to 20 times wider than the impactor itself. Small craters are bowl-shaped, while large ones have central peaks or rings. Craters cover the Moon, Mercury and many moons, which have little or no air, water or geological activity to erase them. On Earth, erosion, plate tectonics and plants hide most craters, but about 200 have been confirmed. The largest known, Vredefort in South Africa, formed about 2 billion years ago and was originally perhaps 160 to 300 kilometers wide. The Chicxulub crater in Mexico, about 180 kilometers wide, formed 66 million years ago by the impact that helped wipe out the dinosaurs. Arizona’s Meteor Crater, about 1.2 kilometers wide, is one of the best preserved.",
s:[["How craters form","On impact, a shock wave compresses and melts rock and the impactor, then rebounds, throwing material outward as ejecta that forms a blanket and rays around the crater. In large impacts the crater floor springs back upward, forming a central peak. Bright rays from the Moon’s crater Tycho stretch for more than 1,000 kilometers."],["Recognizing impact craters","Geologists identify ancient impact sites by features that only extreme shock pressures create, such as shocked quartz, shatter cones and high-pressure minerals like coesite. In 1960, Eugene Shoemaker found coesite at Meteor Crater, proving that it was made by an impact rather than a volcano."],["Famous craters","Meteor Crater in Arizona formed about 50,000 years ago. Popigai in Siberia contains impact diamonds. In 2018, scientists announced a 31-kilometer crater hidden beneath the Greenland ice sheet, named Hiawatha. Manicouagan in Canada forms a ring-shaped lake visible from space."],["Craters elsewhere","Counting craters lets scientists estimate the ages of surfaces on other worlds: heavily cratered areas are old, smooth ones young. The Moon’s dark maria are huge ancient impact basins later flooded with lava. Valhalla on Jupiter’s moon Callisto is a multi-ring basin about 3,800 kilometers across."]],
k:[["Confirmed on Earth","About 200"],["Largest on Earth","Vredefort, South Africa (about 2 billion years old)"],["Dinosaur-era impact","Chicxulub, Mexico (66 million years ago)"],["Best preserved","Meteor Crater, Arizona"],["Proof of impact","Shocked quartz, shatter cones, coesite"]],
d:[["c. 2 billion years ago","Vredefort impact in South Africa"],["66 million years ago","Chicxulub impact"],["c. 50,000 years ago","Meteor Crater forms in Arizona"],["1960","Shoemaker proves Meteor Crater was made by an impact"],["1991","Chicxulub identified as the dinosaur-killing crater"],["2018","Hiawatha crater found under Greenland’s ice"]],
s2:[["Chicxulub","In 1980, Luis and Walter Alvarez proposed that an asteroid impact caused the extinction of the dinosaurs, based on a layer rich in the rare metal iridium. In 1991, scientists linked the idea to a buried crater at Chicxulub on Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula, first noticed by oil company geophysicists."],["Crater lakes and resources","Many impact craters hold lakes, such as Pingualuit in Canada. Some craters contain valuable resources: the Sudbury basin in Canada is a major source of nickel, and oil and gas are found in buried craters."]],
sp:["Eugene Shoemaker","Geologist Eugene Shoemaker founded the science of astrogeology and helped train Apollo astronauts. He hoped to go to the Moon himself; after his death in 1997, some of his ashes were carried to the Moon aboard the Lunar Prospector spacecraft, making him the only person buried there."],
g:[["Impactor","An object that hits another"],["Ejecta","Material thrown out of a crater"],["Shatter cone","A cone-shaped rock fracture made by shock waves"],["Maria","Dark plains on the Moon"]],
f:["About 200 impact craters are confirmed on Earth.","Craters are usually 10–20 times wider than the impactor.","Eugene Shoemaker is the only person with ashes on the Moon."],
fun:"Meteor Crater in Arizona was used to train Apollo astronauts in geology before they went to the Moon."});

ADDART({id:"starship",t:"SpaceX Starship",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"SpaceX Starship",r:["reusablerockets","rocket","artemis","mars","spaceports"],
p:[["The biggest rocket","Starship is the largest and most powerful rocket ever flown."],["Fully reusable design","Both stages are designed to be caught and flown again."],["Moon and Mars","NASA chose a version of Starship to land astronauts on the Moon."]],
l:"Starship is a super heavy-lift launch system developed by the American company SpaceX, designed to be fully reusable. It consists of two stages: the Super Heavy booster, powered by 33 Raptor engines burning liquid methane and liquid oxygen, and the Starship spacecraft, or Ship, on top, which has its own six engines. Stacked together, the system stands about 124 meters tall, making it the largest and most powerful rocket ever flown. SpaceX began full test flights from its Starbase site in southern Texas in April 2023; early flights ended in explosions, but the company improved the design with each test. On 13 October 2024, the launch tower’s mechanical arms caught a returning Super Heavy booster for the first time. The Ship reached orbit for the first time on 28 September 2026, and on 14 November 2026 the tower caught a Ship returning from orbit. NASA selected a Starship lunar lander for its Artemis program, and SpaceX hopes Starship will eventually carry people to Mars.",
s:[["Design","Starship is built mainly of stainless steel, which is cheaper than carbon composites and handles both very cold fuel and the heat of reentry. Thousands of hexagonal heat-shield tiles protect the Ship as it reenters the atmosphere belly-first, then flips upright for landing."],["Testing by flying","SpaceX uses an approach of rapid testing, building and flying prototypes quickly and learning from failures. Several early test flights exploded or broke apart, and debris from some flights fell near the Caribbean, leading to investigations by US aviation authorities."],["Moon lander","In 2021, NASA chose a version of Starship as the Human Landing System for Artemis. To reach the Moon, it must be refueled in Earth orbit by several tanker flights, a technique that has not yet been demonstrated. NASA plans a 2027 test in Earth orbit before a crewed lunar landing."],["Satellites and Mars","Starship is designed to carry more than 100 metric tons to orbit and is used to launch larger Starlink satellites. SpaceX founder Elon Musk has said he wants Starship to build a city on Mars, though no firm schedule exists for crewed Mars missions."]],
k:[["Builder","SpaceX"],["Height","About 124 m"],["Booster engines","33 Raptor engines"],["Fuel","Liquid methane and liquid oxygen"],["First Ship catch","14 November 2026"]],
d:[["2019","Starhopper prototype makes test hops"],["2021","NASA selects Starship as a lunar lander"],["2023","First full Starship test flight"],["2024","First Super Heavy booster caught by tower arms"],["2026","Ship reaches orbit (28 September)"],["2026","Ship caught by tower after orbital flight (14 November)"]],
s2:[["Why methane?","Raptor engines burn methane rather than kerosene because it burns cleaner, leaving fewer deposits in reused engines, and because methane could in principle be made on Mars from carbon dioxide and water ice, providing fuel for return journeys."],["Environmental concerns","Starship launches from Starbase near wildlife refuges on the Texas coast. Environmental groups have raised concerns about noise, debris and road closures, while supporters point to jobs and technological progress."]],
sp:["Chopsticks","SpaceX’s launch towers, nicknamed ‘Mechazilla’, have two huge arms called chopsticks. They swing together to grab a descending booster or ship by small pins, avoiding the weight of landing legs and allowing quick reuse."],
g:[["Super heavy-lift","Able to carry very heavy loads to orbit"],["Stage","A section of a rocket with its own engines"],["Raptor","SpaceX’s methane-fueled rocket engine"],["Refueling in orbit","Transferring fuel between spacecraft in space"]],
f:["Starship is about 124 meters tall.","Its booster has 33 engines.","A tower first caught a returning Ship in November 2026."],
fun:"Starship’s Super Heavy booster produces about twice the thrust of the Saturn V rocket that carried astronauts to the Moon."});

ADDART({id:"megaconstellations",t:"Satellite Megaconstellations",c:"Space Exploration",i:"📡",w:"Satellite constellation",r:["sat","internet","spacejunk","lightpollution","reusablerockets","satellites"],
p:[["Swarms of satellites","Megaconstellations are networks of thousands of satellites."],["Internet from space","Starlink and others provide internet almost anywhere."],["Concerns","They raise worries about debris, astronomy and control of space."]],
l:"A satellite megaconstellation is a network of hundreds or thousands of satellites in low Earth orbit working together, mainly to provide broadband internet around the world. Because they orbit only a few hundred kilometers up, much closer than traditional communications satellites in geostationary orbit about 36,000 kilometers away, they can deliver fast connections with little delay, but many satellites are needed to cover the whole planet. SpaceX’s Starlink, which began launching in 2019, is by far the largest, with more than 10,000 satellites in orbit by 2026, serving millions of users, including ships, aircraft, remote communities and disaster areas; it also became important in the war in Ukraine. Other systems include Eutelsat OneWeb, Amazon’s Project Kuiper and Chinese constellations such as Qianfan and Guowang. Megaconstellations have helped connect remote areas, but astronomers warn that bright satellite trails interfere with telescope images, and experts worry about collisions, space debris and the power of a few companies over global communications.",
s:[["How they work","Users connect with a small dish antenna that tracks satellites as they pass overhead. Satellites relay data to ground stations connected to the internet, and newer satellites pass data between each other using lasers. Each satellite lasts about five years before being lowered to burn up in the atmosphere."],["Starlink","SpaceX launched its first batch of 60 Starlink satellites in May 2019, using its reusable Falcon 9 rockets, and passed 10,000 satellites in orbit in March 2026. Starlink now provides service in more than 100 countries, and some newer satellites can connect directly to ordinary mobile phones."],["Effects on astronomy","Sunlight reflecting off satellites creates streaks across telescope images, especially near dawn and dusk. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory, which began its survey in 2025, is expected to find satellite trails in many images. Companies have darkened satellites, and astronomers develop software to remove streaks, but radio interference is also a concern."],["Debris and traffic","Thousands of satellites in similar orbits must constantly maneuver to avoid collisions. Starlink satellites performed tens of thousands of avoidance maneuvers in recent years. Scientists also study whether metals and other materials from satellites burning up affect the upper atmosphere."]],
k:[["Orbit","Low Earth orbit, a few hundred km up"],["Largest","SpaceX Starlink"],["Starlink first launch","May 2019"],["Starlink satellites in orbit","More than 10,000 (2026)"],["Others","OneWeb, Kuiper, Qianfan, Guowang"]],
d:[["1998","Iridium phone satellite network begins service"],["2019","First 60 Starlink satellites launched"],["2020","Eutelsat OneWeb resumes launches after bankruptcy"],["2022","Starlink terminals sent to Ukraine"],["2025","Amazon launches first Kuiper production satellites"],["2026","Starlink passes 10,000 satellites in orbit"]],
s2:[["Iridium","The Iridium network, completed in 1998, was an early constellation of 66 satellites for satellite phones. The company went bankrupt in 1999 but was revived, and its sun-reflecting antennas once produced brief bright ‘Iridium flares’ in the night sky."],["Satellites and war","After Russia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022, Starlink terminals helped Ukraine keep communications working. The role of a private company in a war raised debates about who controls important infrastructure in space."]],
sp:["Satellite trains","Shortly after launch, Starlink satellites can be seen as a line of bright moving dots, like a ‘train’, crossing the night sky before they spread out and rise to their working orbits. Many people have mistaken them for UFOs."],
g:[["Constellation (satellites)","A group of satellites working together"],["Low Earth orbit","Orbits up to about 2,000 km above Earth"],["Latency","Delay in sending data"],["Ground station","A station on Earth that communicates with satellites"]],
f:["Starlink has more than 10,000 satellites in orbit.","Megaconstellation satellites burn up after about five years.","Satellite streaks affect telescope images."],
fun:"Some Starlink satellites have been fitted with dark visors or special mirror coatings to make them less bright in the night sky."});

ADDART({id:"spacetourism",t:"Space Tourism",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🧑‍🚀",w:"Space tourism",r:["commspace","iss","edgeofspace","reusablerockets","tourism"],
p:[["Paying to fly to space","Space tourism lets private people travel to space for fun."],["Suborbital and orbital","Some trips last minutes; others spend days in orbit."],["Expensive and uncertain","Tickets cost hundreds of thousands to millions of dollars."]],
l:"Space tourism is spaceflight by private individuals for recreation or adventure rather than as professional astronauts. The first space tourist, American businessman Dennis Tito, paid about 20 million dollars to fly to the International Space Station on a Russian Soyuz in April 2001, and several others followed. Suborbital trips, which go just above the boundary of space for a few minutes of weightlessness and views of Earth before returning, began in 2021, when Richard Branson flew on Virgin Galactic’s rocket plane on 11 July and Jeff Bezos on Blue Origin’s New Shepard on 20 July. In September 2021, SpaceX’s Inspiration4 carried the first all-civilian crew into orbit for three days, and in 2024 the private Polaris Dawn mission included the first commercial spacewalk. However, demand and schedules have proved uncertain: Blue Origin paused New Shepard flights in January 2026, and Virgin Galactic has not flown customers since 2024 while it develops new spaceplanes. Critics question the environmental impact and value of space trips for the very rich.",
s:[["Suborbital flights","New Shepard is a reusable rocket and capsule that launches vertically from Texas, carrying six passengers above 100 kilometers, the Kármán line, before parachuting back. Virgin Galactic’s spaceplanes are carried by a mothership aircraft and then fire a rocket to climb to about 85 kilometers, which the United States counts as space."],["Orbital trips","Orbital space tourism costs tens of millions of dollars per seat. Private astronauts have flown to the ISS on Russian Soyuz and SpaceX Crew Dragon spacecraft, including missions organized by the company Axiom Space. Japanese billionaire Yusaku Maezawa spent 12 days on the ISS in December 2021."],["Who goes?","Most space tourists have been wealthy businesspeople, but some flights carried guests chosen through competitions or charities, such as a physician assistant, a teacher and an 82-year-old aviation pioneer, Wally Funk, who had trained as an astronaut in the 1960s. William Shatner, who played Captain Kirk in Star Trek, flew in 2021 at age 90."],["Debates","Supporters say space tourism helps fund reusable rockets and inspires interest in science. Critics point to emissions from rocket launches, safety risks and the cost of flights for a tiny number of people. In 2014, a Virgin Galactic test flight crashed, killing one pilot."]],
k:[["First space tourist","Dennis Tito, 2001"],["First suborbital tourist flights","July 2021"],["First all-civilian orbital crew","Inspiration4, 2021"],["First commercial spacewalk","Polaris Dawn, 2024"],["New Shepard status","Paused since January 2026"]],
d:[["2001","Dennis Tito visits the ISS"],["2004","SpaceShipOne wins the Ansari X Prize"],["2014","Virgin Galactic test flight crash"],["2021","Branson and Bezos fly to space; Inspiration4 orbits Earth"],["2024","Polaris Dawn includes a commercial spacewalk"],["2026","Blue Origin pauses New Shepard flights"]],
s2:[["The X Prize","In 2004, SpaceShipOne, designed by Burt Rutan and funded by Paul Allen, became the first private crewed spacecraft to reach space twice within two weeks, winning the 10 million dollar Ansari X Prize. Its technology led to Virgin Galactic’s spaceplanes."],["Space hotels","Several companies have proposed private space stations and hotels, and NASA is funding commercial stations to replace the ISS, which is planned to be retired around 2030. None had hosted tourists as of 2026."]],
sp:["The overview effect","Many people who see Earth from space report a powerful change in perspective, sensing the planet’s fragility and unity. Writer Frank White called this the ‘overview effect’ in 1987."],
g:[["Suborbital","Reaching space but not completing an orbit"],["Kármán line","A boundary of space at 100 km altitude"],["Weightlessness","The feeling of floating in free fall"],["Private astronaut","A non-professional traveler to space"]],
f:["The first space tourist flew in 2001.","Branson and Bezos flew to space in July 2021.","William Shatner flew to space at age 90."],
fun:"Dennis Tito, the first space tourist, has booked a seat on a future SpaceX flight around the Moon."});

ADDART({id:"moonbases",t:"Moon Bases",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌕",w:"Moonbase",r:["moon","artemis","chandrayaan","starship","apollo","spacemining"],
p:[["Living on the Moon","Space agencies plan permanent bases on the Moon."],["Ice at the poles","Water ice near the lunar south pole could supply water, air and fuel."],["A new race","The United States and China both aim to land astronauts and build bases."]],
l:"A Moon base is a planned permanent or long-term human outpost on the Moon. No humans have been to the Moon since Apollo 17 in 1972, but the United States, China and other countries now plan to return astronauts and eventually build bases. Interest focuses on the lunar south pole, where craters in permanent shadow contain water ice, first confirmed by spacecraft in 2008 and 2009. Ice could be turned into drinking water, breathable oxygen and hydrogen rocket fuel, making long stays and travel deeper into space easier. NASA’s Artemis program aims to land astronauts near the south pole, with a landing currently targeted for 2028 using a commercial lander, followed by a ‘base camp’. China plans to land astronauts before 2030 and, with Russia and other partners, to build an International Lunar Research Station in the 2030s. Challenges include radiation, extreme temperatures, moonquakes, sharp lunar dust and the cost of supplying a base from Earth.",
s:[["Why the south pole?","Near the lunar poles, the Sun stays low on the horizon. Some crater floors never see sunlight and stay colder than −200°C, trapping ice for billions of years, while nearby ridges are lit for most of the year, ideal for solar power. India’s Chandrayaan-3 became the first spacecraft to land near the south pole in August 2023."],["Building on the Moon","Bases could be partly buried under lunar soil, called regolith, to shield astronauts from radiation and micrometeorites. Engineers are testing ways to 3D-print structures from regolith and to extract oxygen from lunar rocks. Lava tubes, natural tunnels left by ancient lava flows, could also provide shelter."],["Plans","NASA’s plans include Artemis landings with SpaceX’s Starship or Blue Origin’s Blue Moon landers, pressurized rovers and surface habitats. China plans a crewed landing with its Mengzhou spacecraft and Lanyue lander before 2030. Both countries have discussed small nuclear reactors to provide power during the two-week lunar night."],["Challenges","Lunar dust is fine and jagged, sticks to everything and can damage equipment and lungs. Temperatures swing from about 120°C in daylight to −130°C at night. Without a magnetic field or atmosphere, astronauts would face radiation from the Sun and deep space, and resupplying a base from Earth is very costly."]],
k:[["Last crewed landing","Apollo 17, 1972"],["Target region","Lunar south pole"],["Key resource","Water ice"],["NASA crewed landing target","2028 (Artemis)"],["China’s target","Before 2030"]],
d:[["1972","Apollo 17, the last crewed Moon landing"],["2008","Chandrayaan-1 detects signs of water on the Moon"],["2009","LCROSS impact confirms ice in a polar crater"],["2017","Artemis program begins"],["2021","China and Russia announce the International Lunar Research Station"],["2023","Chandrayaan-3 lands near the south pole"]],
s2:[["The Outer Space Treaty","The Outer Space Treaty of 1967 says that no country can claim the Moon. The US-led Artemis Accords, signed by dozens of countries since 2020, set principles for using lunar resources, while China and Russia have their own partnership. Experts debate how resources and ‘safety zones’ around bases should be managed."],["Moon bases in fiction","Moon bases have long appeared in science fiction, from H. G. Wells’s The First Men in the Moon (1901) to the 1970s television series Space: 1999 and the film Moon (2009)."]],
sp:["LCROSS","In October 2009, NASA deliberately crashed a spent rocket stage into Cabeus crater near the lunar south pole. The LCROSS spacecraft flew through the plume of debris and detected water, confirming ice in permanently shadowed craters."],
g:[["Regolith","Loose soil and rock on the Moon’s surface"],["Permanently shadowed crater","A crater floor that never receives sunlight"],["Lava tube","A natural tunnel formed by flowing lava"],["Lunar night","About two weeks of darkness on the Moon"]],
f:["No one has walked on the Moon since 1972.","Ice near the lunar south pole could make rocket fuel.","Chandrayaan-3 landed near the south pole in 2023."],
fun:"A day-night cycle on the Moon lasts about 29.5 Earth days, so a lunar base would have about two weeks of sunlight followed by two weeks of darkness."});
